Equivalence Groups Found
└── finite-state machine engine for managing states/transitions
    ├── Candidates : transitions, python-statemachine, automat
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine, add_transition, model injection (state, trip, etc.), 
    │   conditions=, after=, wildcard '*' transitions
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions' Machine(model=self,...) dynamically attaches methods 
    │   (start_fill, trip, etc.) to the instance; python-statemachine and automat 
    │   require states/transitions to be declared as class attributes/decorators on the
    │   owning class itself, not injected post-hoc; conditions= and after= callback 
    │   string-lookup style (looking up self.can_fill by name) has no direct equivalent
    │   in automat, which uses explicit MethodicalMachine decorators; wildcard '*' 
    │   source state transitions (used for 'trip') are a transitions-specific 
    │   convenience; must be manually expanded to enumerate all source states in most 
    │   alternatives; state string attribute self.state is transitions-specific; other 
    │   libraries expose current state differently (e.g. state_machine.current_state)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions is used here as a generic FSM engine that dynamically injects 
        transition methods (start_fill, trip, etc.) onto the TankController instance 
        via Machine(model=self, ...). python-statemachine and automat are the closest 
        widely-used Python FSM libraries that could conceptually replace this role 
        (declarative state/transition management with guard conditions and callbacks), 
        but neither uses the same 'model injection' pattern -- both require class-based
        state/transition declarations rather than add_transition() calls on an external
        Machine object bound to a model.

