constructor. Its core class, minimalmodbus.Instr
  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - minimalmodbus is fundamentally a serial (RTU/ASCII over RS-485/RS-232) library; 
it has no native TCP client analogous to pymodbus.ModbusTcpClient(host, port). No 
wrapper can conjure network transport that the target library does not implement.
    - pymodbus's client.connect() has no equivalent in minimalmodbus — Instrument opens
the serial port implicitly on first communication; there's no explicit 
connect/disconnect lifecycle to wrap onto a TCP host string.
    - pymodbus passes `unit=` (slave id) per call to 
read_holding_registers/write_register; minimalmodbus binds the slave address once at 
Instrument construction, requiring a structural redesign of 
ModbusIO.__init__/_read1/write_valve, not just a call-site wrapper.
    - pymodbus returns response objects exposing .isError() and .registers; 
minimalmodbus's read_register/read_registers/write_register return raw values directly 
and raise exceptions (e.g. minimalmodbus.ModbusException, serial.SerialException) on 
failure. This changes the error-handling model pervasively across _read1 and 
write_valve, beyond what a single glue wrapper at the client-construction call site can
fix.
    - Overall: the migration depends on a client abstraction (network Modbus TCP 
master) that minimalmodbus does not provide any path to support; this is a structural 
transport-layer gap, not an API-shape mismatch that wrapper glue code can close.

